#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:1
msgid "What is rustdoc?"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:3
msgid ""
"The standard Rust distribution ships with a tool called `rustdoc`. Its job "
"is to generate documentation for Rust projects. On a fundamental level, "
"Rustdoc takes as an argument either a crate root or a Markdown file, and "
"produces HTML, CSS, and JavaScript."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:8
msgid "Basic usage"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:10
msgid "Let's give it a try! Create a new project with Cargo:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:17
msgid ""
"In `src/lib.rs`, Cargo has generated some sample code. Delete it and replace "
"it with this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:21 src/what-is-rustdoc.md:61
msgid "/// foo is a function\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:25
msgid ""
"Let's run `rustdoc` on our code. To do so, we can call it with the path to "
"our crate root like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:32
msgid ""
"This will create a new directory, `doc`, with a website inside! In our case, "
"the main page is located in `doc/lib/index.html`. If you open that up in a "
"web browser, you will see a page with a search bar, and \"Crate lib\" at the "
"top, with no contents."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:37
msgid ""
"You can also use `cargo doc` to generate documentation for the whole "
"project. See [Using rustdoc with Cargo](#using-rustdoc-with-cargo)."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:40
msgid "Configuring rustdoc"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:42
msgid ""
"There are two problems with this: first, why does it think that our crate is "
"named \"lib\"? Second, why does it not have any contents?"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:46
msgid ""
"The first problem is due to `rustdoc` trying to be helpful; like `rustc`, it "
"assumes that our crate's name is the name of the file for the crate root. To "
"fix this, we can pass in a command-line flag:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:54
msgid ""
"Now, `doc/docs/index.html` will be generated, and the page says \"Crate docs."
"\""
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:56
msgid ""
"For the second issue, it is because our function `foo` is not public; "
"`rustdoc` defaults to generating documentation for only public functions. If "
"we change our code..."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:65
msgid "... and then re-run `rustdoc`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:71
msgid ""
"We now have some generated documentation. Open up `doc/docs/index.html` and "
"check it out! It should show a link to the `foo` function's page, which is "
"located at `doc/docs/fn.foo.html`. On that page, you'll see the \"foo is a "
"function\" we put inside the documentation comment in our crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:76
msgid "Using rustdoc with Cargo"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:78
msgid ""
"Cargo also has integration with `rustdoc` to make it easier to generate "
"docs. Instead of the `rustdoc` command, we could have done this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:85
msgid ""
"If you want `cargo` to automatically open the generated documentation, you "
"can use:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:91
msgid "Internally, `cargo doc` calls out to `rustdoc` like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:95
msgid "<path>/docs/target/debug/deps"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:98
msgid "You can see this with `cargo doc --verbose`."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:100
msgid ""
"It generates the correct `--crate-name` for us, as well as pointing to `src/"
"lib.rs`. But what about those other arguments?"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:102
msgid ""
"`-o` controls the _o_utput of our docs. Instead of a top-level `doc` "
"directory, notice that Cargo puts generated documentation under `target`. "
"That is the idiomatic place for generated files in Cargo projects."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:105
msgid ""
"`-L` flag helps rustdoc find the dependencies your code relies on. If our "
"project used dependencies, we would get documentation for them as well!"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:108
msgid "Outer and inner documentation"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:110
msgid ""
"The `///` syntax is used to document the item present after it. That's why "
"it is called an outer documentation. There is another syntax: `//!`, which "
"is used to document the item it is present inside. It is called an inner "
"documentation. It is often used when documenting the entire crate, because "
"nothing comes before it: it is the root of the crate. So in order to "
"document an entire crate, you need to use `//!` syntax. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:120
msgid "//! This is my first rust crate\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:123
msgid ""
"When used in the crate root, it documents the item it is inside, which is "
"the crate itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:126
msgid ""
"For more information about the `//!` syntax, see [the Book](https://doc.rust-"
"lang.org/book/ch14-02-publishing-to-crates-io.html#commenting-contained-"
"items)."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:131
msgid "Using standalone Markdown files"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:133
msgid ""
"`rustdoc` can also generate HTML from standalone Markdown files. Let' s give "
"it a try: create a `README.md` file with these contents:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:136
msgid ""
"````text\n"
"# Docs\n"
"\n"
"This is a project to test out `rustdoc`.\n"
"\n"
"[Here is a link!](https://www.rust-lang.org)\n"
"\n"
"## Example\n"
"\n"
"```rust\n"
"fn foo() -> i32 {\n"
"    1 + 1\n"
"}\n"
"```\n"
"````"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:152
msgid "And call `rustdoc` on it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:158
msgid ""
"You will find an HTML file in `docs/doc/README.html` generated from its "
"Markdown contents."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:161
msgid ""
"Cargo currently does not understand standalone Markdown files, unfortunately."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:163
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:165
msgid ""
"This covers the simplest use-cases of `rustdoc`. The rest of this book will "
"explain all of the options that `rustdoc` has, and how to use them."
msgstr ""
